>>>... 2) Mass assigment vulnerabilites
- aka Auto-Binding
- using internal object we can create/update hidden parameters

Internal Object

		User {
			id,
			name,
			email,
			isAdmin
		}

The developer expects users to change only:

		{
		"name": "Yash",
		"email": "yash@example.com"
		}

But because of mass assignment, an attacker can send:

		{
		"name": "Yash",
		"email": "yash@example.com",
		"isAdmin": true
		}

The framework automatically updates:

		user.isAdmin = true;



				Find API Endpoint
					↓
				Inspect Response Object
					↓
				Guess Hidden Fields
				(isAdmin, role, price, discount, credit, etc.)
					↓
				Test PATCH / PUT Request
					↓
				Send Hidden Parameter
					↓
				Framework Auto-Binds Data
					↓
				Internal Object Updated
					↓
				Privilege / Business Logic Abuse


>>>... 3) Server-side parameter pollution
- It happens when:
	- A user sends input to a website.
	- The website uses that input to make a request to an internal API.
	- The website does not properly sanitize or encode the input.
	- The attacker injects extra parameters into the internal API request.


-> Testing for server-side parameter pollution in the query string
- place the query string characters in the internal API and observer the response
